## Amendment 2026-08-21: the sqlite-vec ruling is reversed

The body below rules `sqlite-vec` out because "it is a loadable extension, and
the point of `node:sqlite` here is to add no dependency". **That ruling rested
on a false premise and the author has reversed it.** Verified directly:

- `node:sqlite` does support loadable extensions -- `DatabaseSync` accepts
  `{ allowExtension: true }`, and `enableLoadExtension` / `loadExtension` both
  work on Node 22.23.1.
- `sqlite-vec` is an ordinary npm install: v0.1.9, prebuilt per-platform binaries shipped as its own
  `optionalDependencies`, no compilation, MIT/Apache. Size depends on what is
  measured: 188 kB on disk for the wrapper plus the linux-x64 package, of which
  the native binary is 160 160 bytes (156 KiB) unpacked -- the figure 0008 uses
  against sqlite-vss's 5,1 MB. A KNN query was run through it end to end.
- zoteus already uses exactly this pattern twice: `pdfjs-dist` in
  `optionalDependencies`, and `@huggingface/transformers` resolved without
  executing in `embeddings.ts`, degrading gracefully. Vectors exist only when
  that far heavier optional dependency is installed, so `sqlite-vec` is needed
  precisely when one already is.

What this buys is the reason it was accepted: KNN runs in C, so `mode:
"semantic"` keeps upstream's exact recall with no JS scan. The alternative --
a faithful full scan in JS -- would pull roughly 628 MB through memory per
query at 384 dims over 408 628 passages, partly reintroducing the ceiling this
chantier exists to remove. The other alternative, restricting the scan to FTS5
candidates, bounds memory but makes a semantically-close passage sharing no
keyword with the query unreachable, which is the case semantic search is for.

**Embedding is NOT delegated to SQLite.** `sqlite-lembed` (0.0.1-alpha.8) and
`sqlite-rembed` (0.0.1-alpha.9) stay out. They generate embeddings, which is a
different job from storing them: swapping the model changes the vectors and so
voids 0001's "same corpus in, same results out" gate, they are alpha where
`sqlite-vec` is released, embedding is not the bottleneck the measurements
show, and replacing the provider would make this something other than the
contained storage swap the upstream posture depends on. `sqlite-rembed` as a
remote-embedding *feature* is a separate ticket if wanted.

**One wrinkle this creates.** `vec0` needs the vector dimension at CREATE time;
zoteus discovers it at runtime from the tensor and it varies by model. So the
vec table is created lazily on the first vector, its dimension recorded in the
`index_meta` table 0005 added, and read back on reopen. A later vector of a
different length (the user changed `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDINGS`) must not be stored
beside the others -- mixed dimensions in one table produce plausible nonsense.

## Context

Vectors currently live in a JS array beside the keyword index and are fused
with BM25 by reciprocal-rank fusion in process. In SQLite they become a BLOB
column, and the fusion has to be reimplemented against FTS5's `bm25()`.
Tracker: 0001.

Note the sign: FTS5's `bm25()` returns a negative score, best-first ascending.
Fusing it with cosine similarity without normalising the direction inverts the
ranking, and the suite would not necessarily catch it.

## Design, planned against 0002's port

0002's `PassageStore` already owns the passage row; the embedding belongs on
the same row, so it joins the port rather than living in a parallel structure:

    setVector(id: string, v: Float32Array): void
    vectorSearch(q: Float32Array, topK: number): Array<{ id: string; score: number }>
    readonly vectorCount: number

`MemoryPassageStore` delegates all three to the existing `VectorStore`, so the
default path keeps today's code and today's numbers. `Fts5PassageStore` adds a
`vec BLOB` column to `passage_meta` and one `dim INTEGER` beside it -- a bare
BLOB with no recorded dimension is unreadable once a second embedder has ever
been configured.

**The sign trap, stated once so it cannot be missed.** FTS5's `bm25()` returns
a negative score, best-first ascending; cosine similarity is positive,
best-first descending. 0002 already negates `bm25()` at the store boundary, so
by the time `rrf()` sees either list both are descending-best. Do not negate
twice. The existing `rrf()` ranks by list *position* and ignores magnitude,
which is what makes fusing two incomparable scales legitimate at all -- the
danger is not the scale, it is a list sorted the wrong way round, and the suite
would not necessarily catch it. Assert the direction directly: a test where the
known-best passage for a query is asserted to be at index 0 of the raw
`vectorSearch` and `search` results, before any fusion.

**Retrieval, then rescore in JS.** Candidates come from FTS5's MATCH (and, for
`mode: "semantic"`, from a full scan of the vector column), then cosine is
computed in process. `sqlite-vec` stays out of scope: it is a loadable
extension, and the point of `node:sqlite` here is to add no dependency.

At 384 dims float32 that is 1,536 B/passage, so 408 628 passages is ~628 MB of
BLOB. That is disk, not RSS -- but `mode: "semantic"` as described scans every
vector, which would pull all of it through memory per query. Measure it; if it
bites, the answer is to restrict the scan to FTS5 candidates and accept that
pure-semantic recall differs from upstream, which is a decision to bring back
to the author rather than take here.

## Actions

1. `setVector`/`vectorSearch`/`vectorCount` on `PassageStore`; memory delegates
   to `VectorStore`, FTS5 adds `vec BLOB` + `dim INTEGER` to `passage_meta`.
2. Candidate retrieval from FTS5, cosine rescoring in JS.
3. Fuse with the existing `rrf()`, both lists already descending-best.
4. Measure BLOB storage and per-query RSS on the full library; record in `bench/`.

## Verification

- [x] Same query, same top-k as the current implementation on a shared sample
- [ ] Embedding storage measured on the real library -- BLOCKED, needs the
      author's 7 540-item corpus; command handed over. Synthetic figure taken:
      ~1,59-1,70 kB/passage at 384 dims. Ticked boxes on this ticket mean done,
