## Findings from the implementation

**The direction guard took two sabotages to test properly, and the first was a
bad test.** Reversing `ORDER BY v.distance` reddened 19 tests -- but vec0
rejects that at `prepare()` time, so it broke the mechanism rather than the
ordering, which proves nothing about the guard. The honest sabotage is
`.reverse()` on the returned hits: **3** tests red -- the two direct
nearest-first/farthest-last and topK assertions in `passage-store.test.ts`, and
`sqlite-index.test.ts`'s reopened-file semantic round trip. (The executor
reported 2; a review reproduction found the third. Corrected 2026-08-21.) Every fused test, every mode test and
every parity test stayed green -- including `semantic mode answers from the
vectors alone`. That is this ticket's own warning demonstrated: a fused-only
assertion cannot see a reversed list, because `rrf()` ranks by position and a
reversed list is still a list.

**Mismatched embedding dimensions throw, naming both widths.** Safe because
`clear()` *drops* the `vec0` table rather than emptying it and forgets the
recorded dimension, and every build begins with `reset()` -> `clear()`. So the
legitimate route to a new width -- change `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDINGS`, rebuild -- never
reaches the throw. What does reach it is one embedder emitting two widths
inside a single build, which is an incoherence rather than a configuration
change; a silent drop-and-rebuild there would discard every vector already
stored, mid-build, without saying so.

**`node:sqlite` binds every JS number as SQLite REAL**, and `vec0` requires an
INTEGER primary key. `INSERT INTO vec0(rowid, ...)` fails with "Only integers
are allows for primary key values" until the rowid is bound as a `BigInt`. The
one non-obvious mechanical fact in the change.

**`vectorEntries()` had to become a required port member**, not a memory-side
detail: 0002/0003 already run `toJSON`/`loadFromJSON` round trips through
`describe.each(STORES)` against a bare `Fts5PassageStore`, and keeping the dump
memory-only turned both red. The heap concern is untouched --
`SqliteSearchIndex.toJSON()` still refuses the whole snapshot before reaching
it.

**A `score > 0` filter was added to `Fts5PassageStore.vectorSearch`**, beyond
the ticket's "convert distance to similarity". Without it the stores diverge:
`VectorStore` drops orthogonal and opposed passages, `vec0` would rank them
last. The parameterised suites hold both to the same assertions, so parity is
not optional.

**Residual, deliberate:** `MemoryPassageStore.setVector` *appends* on a
repeated id, since its `VectorStore` has no key index, while the FTS5 store
replaces. Left as a pure delegate because no live path embeds the same id
twice.

**The performance fact the ticket did not anticipate**, and which 0008 now
carries: `sqlite-vec` 0.1.x has no ANN index, so `vec0` KNN is brute force --
fast, in C, on disk, but linear. Measured flat at ~141 ms per 100 000 vectors
across a 40x range. Synthetic measurements at 384 dims: 1 697 B/passage at
20 000, 1 587 B at 100 000, confirming the ticket's ~1,5 kB estimate; 25,7 ms
and 121,8 ms per query respectively. Against the resident `VectorStore` on the
same 20 000: 3 188 B/vector of *live heap* and 57,3 ms/query. So vec0 is 2,2x
faster at zero resident heap, and quantization (0008) takes another 13x.

## Exit criteria

- [x] `mode: "semantic"` and `mode: "auto"` behave as upstream
- [x] Vector storage does not reintroduce a memory ceiling -- zero resident
      heap; the scan is on-disk and in C
- [x] Ranking direction asserted directly, not only through fused output
